# The Madras Appropriation Bill, 1957
Tamil title: சென்னை நிதி ஒதுக்கச் சட்ட முன்முடிவு - 1957

Speech delivered on 26 July 1957.

> Creating revenue, acquiring wealth, preserving it, protecting what is preserved, and distributing it wisely—such is able government.

Thus the great Valluvar counsels the State. It is the Government's duty to plan and spend the country's revenue for the people's welfare. Plans follow the Government's policies. If its policies contradict one another, its plans become not only ineffective but meaningless. In this debate on the Appropriation Bill, Anna explains the Government's policy contradictions.

He also prophetically warns, ten years in advance, that rulers who drew excessive interest upon the capital of goodwill accorded to them as heirs of freedom fighters would eventually lose even their principal. Was not Anna a prophet in politics?

**Thiru C. N. Annadurai:** Mr. Speaker, for many days this House has debated various questions with exceptional interest. Today, at the final stage, the customary act of “entrusting” the funds is taking place. I have already said that a Cabinet worthy of the confidence needed to entrust these sums exists today, that it possesses good intentions, and that it possesses ability.

My principal criticism, however, is that people of goodwill and ability have become subject to an unfortunate political environment that obstructs their power of independent thought. Had they lacked ability, I would not have regretted to the same degree their lack of authority. We have in our State Government people with the necessary ability and good intentions. They take genuine care to understand the people's grievances, possess skill in finding ways to remedy them, and have the generosity to welcome views expressed by the opposition when those views stimulate that skill. Yet today's political environment prevents them from acting through independent thought. That truly concerns me.

I do not believe the ruling party wholly denies the defects in the administrative machinery pointed out by many hon. members. I genuinely believe the rulers are prepared to take corrective action and to obtain the cooperation of the opposition in doing so. What I wish to stress here is the contradiction within the rulers' policies.

Viewed generally, their policies contain the broad aim of giving the country good government capable of advancing the people's welfare. But when that aim is put into practice, numerous policy contradictions appear in their administration. In identifying those contradictions, Mr. Speaker, I rely not only on facts known to me but on various views expressed in this House by Congress members themselves.

At the very time the rulers honour and proclaim the policy that land belongs to the tiller, they also insist upon “mechanised agriculture”—that large agricultural farms should be developed scientifically. Anyone who has studied elementary economics will understand that these policies contradict one another, and that a programme of land to the tiller and a programme of mechanised agriculture cannot both be implemented simultaneously. It is because this major contradiction exists in their policy that, although our rulers show affection and concern for cultivators, they are unable to restrain the great landlords.

For a very long time, the people of this country waited for the rulers to control a few large capitalists, like the proverbial parrot waiting for the silk-cotton pod to ripen. They thought: “After the elections the rulers will acquire new strength. Let the next election come; once it is over, the rulers will again gain new strength.” Thus they waited for land reform to arrive.

Meanwhile, as Acharya Vinoba Bhave so aptly pointed out, it is a secret known to the people that great landlords have created all sorts of arrangements so that, even if a law limiting landholdings is enacted, it cannot control them.

If we next consider industry, there too, just as in agriculture, the rulers support both the private and public sectors. When asked why, they say: “Is coexistence not our very policy? We practise coexistence in industry too.”

If “coexistence” was devised to prevent conflict in the world and establish goodwill between one country and another, it is welcome in that setting. But consider a condition in which corpulent capitalists stand on one side and poor workers on the other; great heads of monasteries on one side and mendicants living in servitude to them on the other; mill owners on one side and workers squeezed like sugarcane on the other. If the rulers preserve such contradictions and call it a policy of coexistence, then however much administrative skill, knowledge and ability they possess, and however much love and concern they have for the people, they cannot do the people as much good as is needed.

Although this Government has the good intention of doing a great deal for the people, circumstances today prevent it from fulfilling that intention. To explain this through you, Mr. Speaker, I would not compare these rulers to a doctor. I hope they will not be offended if I compare them to compounders. They are good compounders.

When a patient comes, they look upon the patient with compassion. They try to diagnose the illness and want to cure the disease they discover. But the bottles supplied by the doctor for whom they work contain much water and little medicine. Converting their resulting sorrow into a smile, they give the patient whatever medicine is available.

I genuinely believe the contradictions and deficiencies I have mentioned exist because today's rulers are in this position. Perhaps that is why Sri Rajagopalachari, who once adorned this House in person and now adorns it in portrait form, observed that these State Governments wait for grants from Delhi like cooperative institutions waiting for donations.

We impress upon the rulers this new idea growing in the country. If they disregard it because it comes from the most backward sections of the population, they will meet a miserable fate. It will be like deaf men at a railway station who sleep without hearing the bell as the train departs, then panic after it has gone. When good schemes remain unimplemented and the people are thrown into turmoil, the rulers too will be plunged into distress. I wish to warn them of that.

Today, however good the plans the rulers devise for industry, the country's people cannot benefit. For the past ten years, before the mind's eye of the people, old Manchesters were to depart and new Manchesters arrive; old Lancashires were to go and new Lancashires take their place; old Sheffields were to disappear and new Sheffields arise. They expected all deserts to be made into gardens. As a Tamil poet sang, “Every desert must become a grove,” the people expected the deserts to turn into fertile gardens.

But what is happening? The gardens are all becoming deserts. The people want to discover who abetted this offence and catch the culprit. Today they may lack the power and skill needed to identify the culprit. But if one day they acquire the necessary strength and realise that these rulers were complicit, they will truly grieve and say, “Could people so good have taken part in something so terribly wrong?”

I value one great asset possessed by today's rulers. They deposited in a national bank the goodwill the people gave them during the struggle for national freedom. Over ten years they have drawn lakhs of rupees from that bank. They will not be able to draw so much in the future. Even the fame earned for them by Pandit Nehru will no longer add funds to the bank. The rulers must therefore take the measures necessary to implement good plans successfully.

To frame and carry out development schemes, the rulers must follow policies free of contradiction. The people have complete confidence that they will implement good schemes properly. To execute plans that advance the country, the rulers possess crores of rupees, valuable materials, natural resources, and, more precious than all of these, the people's labour.

When the responsibility for framing and implementing good plans rests upon these rulers under such circumstances, the demand of those on this side is that the rulers must obtain an administrative order in which they can act with pure conviction. With that, I conclude my speech. Greetings.

---NOTES---
The OCR header gives the impossible date “86.07.1957”; the filename `nithi_othukka260757` and the visible fiscal context support 26 July 1957. The subject line's “1927” is likewise treated as 1957. The introductory Kural is source editorial matter preceding Anna's speech and is translated along with the work. The embedded English in Rajagopalachari's quoted observation is illegible in OCR; the translation follows the immediately legible Tamil explanation rather than reconstructing the lost English wording.
